In this lesson we'll look at how to tame three-dimensional problems by chopping them into two familiar right triangles. Here's the big idea. Most 3D questions are really hiding two right triangles, one stacked on top of the other. First, picture the base, the floor of the shape, and find a right triangle lying flat there. That gives you a length, usually a diagonal across the base. Then take that length and tip it upright. It becomes a leg in a second right triangle, this one standing in a vertical plane, going from the floor up to your point in the air. Now you just solve that second triangle. If you're after a straight line through space, like a diagonal inside the solid, you use Pythagoras twice, once on the base, then again to lift it up. If you're after an angle of elevation, say how steeply you're looking up at a point, you keep that base diagonal as a side and use sine, cosine, or tangent in the vertical triangle to find the angle. And here's a neat shortcut. For a rectangular box with sides a, b, and c, the longest diagonal across the inside is the square root of a squared plus b squared plus c squared. So remember, slice the solid into a flat triangle and a standing triangle, then solve each in turn. Now let's see it in action.